  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1021 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nAs the aforementioned shortwave continues east and deepens, a sfc\nlow is expected to be over the Midwest on Fri, and the associated\nfront pushes south into OK and the TX Panhandle on Fri/Sat. \nMeanwhile, the upper ridge remains strong but shifts west, with \nmodest height falls locally, which will be less detrimental to \nprecip development. Models vary in the exact timing of the front, \nbut expect a wind shift to northeasterly at some point Fri-Fri \nnight.\n\nBetween the front itself, as well as NW flow aloft on the east\nside of the upper ridge acting to guide storms off the higher \nterrain towards W TX, there is a decent signal for scattered \nshowers and storms Fri (20-60% chance, highest on the Caprock). \nPWAT values are expected to be seasonably high, approaching 1.5\" \n(90-95th percentile). Forecast soundings also show the typical \ninverted-V signature during the afternoon/evening. Thus, locally \nheavy rain and strong wind gusts will be the main concerns with \nany storms Friday. The latest run of the REFS depicts localized \nrainfall amounts of 1-2\", and there is a corresponding marginal \nrisk of excessive rainfall. Storm chances linger on Sat, but the \ncoverage is not expected to be as high as Fri.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, temps are expected to be marginally\ncooler over the weekend, with highs in the mid to upper 90s. Into \nnext week, the upper pattern features a progressive polar jet \nstream, while persistent upper ridging persists over the Four \nCorners and southern Plains. This pattern should allow above \nnormal temps and mostly dry conditions to persist for much of next\nweek. There is a 50-70% chance for triple digit temps each day \noff the Caprock, but just a 10-30% chance on the Caprock.",
